  "facts": [
    "The George Rogers Clark Memorial Bridge, opened October 31, 1929, was the first Ohio River crossing built from the center outward, a pioneering method later used for San Francisco’s Oakland Bay Bridge.",
    "Designed by Ralph Modjeski and Frank Masters with Paul Philippe Cret’s architectural flair, the bridge’s four-lane cantilevered truss cost $4.7 million and carried tolls until 1946, when bonds were fully redeemed.",
    "In 2024 a Sysco semi‑truck crash left a cab dangling over the river, prompting a month‑long closure and highlighting the bridge’s ongoing structural vigilance after a 2014 cracked girder discovery."
  ],
